/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 309 0 R
/T (This is the most acute risk hypothesis, and the structural mechanism generating the risk is clear. When platforms decompose legal work into modular tasks, clause identification, regulatory checklist completion, contract templating, document annotation, and assign them through algorithmic matching, the question of whether any individual task constitutes "legal advice" becomes genuinely difficult to answer. A data annotator classifying contract clauses is not, in isolation, giving legal advice. But the aggregate output of their work delivers a service clients reasonably understand as legal guidance. The platform positions itself as a technology intermediary; no licensed professional may have meaningfully supervised the process.)
/E (This is the most acute risk hypothesis, and the structural mechanism generating the risk is clear. When platforms decompose legal work into modular tasks, clause identification, regulatory checklist completion, contract templating, document annotation, and assign them through algorithmic matching, the question of whether any individual task constitutes "legal advice" becomes genuinely difficult to answer. A data annotator classifying contract clauses is not, in isolation, giving legal advice. But the aggregate output of their work delivers a service clients reasonably understand as legal guidance. The platform positions itself as a technology intermediary; no licensed professional may have meaningfully supervised the process.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1043 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 309 0 R
/T (Bar Council of India rules formally restrict legal practice to enrolled advocates, and professional responsibility remains with the individual worker. But enforcement against distributed, platform-mediated arrangements is practically untested. Platforms disclaim responsibility as intermediaries; individual gig workers may lack licensure; and the client,  often a lower-income litigant seeking affordable legal help,  has no reliable way to assess the gap in accountability underlying the transaction.)
/E (Bar Council of India rules formally restrict legal practice to enrolled advocates, and professional responsibility remains with the individual worker. But enforcement against distributed, platform-mediated arrangements is practically untested. Platforms disclaim responsibility as intermediaries; individual gig workers may lack licensure; and the client,  often a lower-income litigant seeking affordable legal help,  has no reliable way to assess the gap in accountability underlying the transaction.)
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
